    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey, we're having some weird issues with the sublimation line here. the colors aren't transferring right on some of the polyester blends—they look faded or patchy even though the heat press settings are correct. can u help me troubleshoot why the ink might not be transferring fully?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Sublimation lines can be a bit finicky, especially when dealing with polyester blends. It's not uncommon for the ink to fade or appear patchy, especially if the fabric is prone to \"gobbling up\" the ink. You might want to try adjusting the heat press settings to see if that makes a difference. However, I've found that sometimes the issue lies with the ink itself, and it's not just a matter of heat transfer. It's a bit like trying to get a goblin to cooperate – sometimes they just don't want to play along. \n\nThat being said, let's try to troubleshoot the issue. Have you checked the ink's viscosity and flow rate? If the ink is too thick, it might not transfer evenly, resulting in those pesky patches. You might also want to consider using a different sublimation ink, as some may be more prone to fading or bleeding than others. \n\nAnother thing to keep in mind is the type of polyester blend you're using. Some blends can be more finicky than others, and it's possible that the ink is just having trouble \"gripping\" onto the fabric. In that case, you might want to try using a different type of polyester",
      "source": "target",
